You can also read it or listen to it on its own on the AI Briefing webpage,

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:00.180
where you'll find the written edition in every past episode.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:02.400
The link is in the show notes.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:10.980
Thanks, Michael.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:17.160
This is the AI briefing weekly deep dive for Saturday, November 7, proving AI ROI

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.920
Does outside counsel spend actually fall?

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:24.520
I'm Bella, and with me is Michael, whose job this week is to push back

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:29.160
Like the briefing, it is written and read by AI from public sources

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:37.820
Harvey, the legal AI company, published a post profiling how the legal teams at Bitfury

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:42.780
ICL Group and SUSE are using its platform to expand capacity.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:48.500
One of them, Bitfury, puts a number on what every vertical AI company wants to claim,

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.120
less reliance on outside counsel.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:55.520
And they're big numbers.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.020
Bitfury, a former technology provider turned investment firm,

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:05.460
says it reduced its reliance on outside counsel by more than 50%.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:10.460
ICL Group, a specialty minerals company, says 20%.

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:16.000
That range is why we're giving it a full episode.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:21.120
Outside counsel spend is a real line on a company's profit and loss statement.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:26.120
If AI moves that line, a legal tool stops being a nice-to-have

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:29.940
and becomes a budget a general counsel can defend to the CFO.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:36.040
Here's my problem before we even start, Bella.

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:40.720
These are three accounts Harvey chose to feature on Harvey's own blog.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:43.840
The word they use is reliance, not spend.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.100
Nobody has shown an invoice.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:51.860
Fair, and that's the question for the whole episode.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:56.440
What evidence would actually show AI replacing billable outside work?

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:02.060
Invoices before and after, a change in the mix of matters, or survey estimates.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.920
Survey estimates are mostly what we have.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.000
And it goes well beyond legal.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:13.940
Any vertical AI company selling against a cost line,

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:19.420
whether that's contractors, agencies, or consultants, has the same proof problem.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:21.860
The cost line is easy to see.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.540
What would have happened without the tool is not.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:28.760
The timing helps.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.660
This year's independent spend reports are out,

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:35.720
so we can set Harvey's stories against data that doesn't come from a vendor.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:39.720
First, let's be clear about what the claim actually is.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:45.100
Start with how it's supposed to work, because the logic is sound.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:50.460
A small in-house team often has the expertise for routine drafting and diligence,

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:51.700
but not the hours.

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:55.480
So that work goes to a law firm, billed by the hour.

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:01.420
So the AI is standing in for junior associate hours at the firm.

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:04.400
That's the claim.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:13.180
According to Harvey's post, Bitfury's team now uses the tool to bring transaction documents roughly 80% of the way to a final draft.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:17.860
The outside firm then reviews the deal instead of building the work from scratch.

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:22.820
They do the same with data rooms during due diligence.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:32.900
The lawyers also use it for a first understanding of foreign regulations, which used to mean hiring local counsel every time a deal entered a new country.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:42.680
I believe the workflow. As an engineer, I'd expect exactly that split. The machine drafts, a human reviews.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:51.460
What I don't understand is what a 50% cut in reliance means as a measurement. Is it dollars, hours, or matters sent out?

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:59.540
The post doesn't say, it's an outcome with no unit attached. ICL's 20% is the same.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:06.200
Then let's make it concrete, because I think the unit is where this falls apart.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.300
Here's a worked example. Did AI cut its spend in half?

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:15.020
Depends what happened to the work.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:22.700
Exactly. Suppose last year included a big acquisition and a lawsuit, and this year had neither.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:26.180
Spend would have fallen anyway, tool or no tool.

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:29.160
Now flip it.

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:32.280
That's a real saving the invoice total hides completely.

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:38.780
So a raw comparison of invoices can mislead in either direction.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:43.980
Yes, an honest measurement adjusts for the mix of matters.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:50.300
You compare like with like, deals against deals and disputes against disputes, before and after.

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:53.420
Energy efficiency firms have the same problem.

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:59.900
A building's bill drops in a mild winter, so they adjust for weather before claiming the retrofit worked.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:02.760
Good analogy.

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:07.700
Here the weather is deal flow, litigation, and regulatory surprises.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:12.740
But that means all the judgment sits in the estimate of what would have happened otherwise.

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:14.560
Who makes that estimate?

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:18.860
In these stories, the customer does.

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:28.460
SUSE, the open-source software company, says it saved up to £80,000 in external fees on one acquisition's due diligence.

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:34.680
Look at ICL's range. The top is nearly double the bottom.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:40.120
That tells you it's a guess at what a firm would have charged, not a number off an invoice.

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:43.940
I'd put it a bit more kindly, Michael.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:49.280
It's an estimate of what a firm would have bid, which legal ops teams do all the time.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:52.820
It's a real method. It just isn't a measured saving.

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:59.800
Agreed, and I'll concede that the single-matter version is the strongest evidence here.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.940
One defined deal with a known scope, done in-house.

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:08.480
That's much closer to a controlled comparison than a reliance percentage.

00:14:10.280 --> 00:14:11.940
There's a second subtlety.

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:14.600
Capacity and spend are different things.

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:19.640
SUSE reports nearly 1,500 hours saved over four months.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:24.960
ICL says the tool gave its core team the equivalent of an extra full-time lawyer.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:29.360
And neither of those is outside spend.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.680
Hours saved inside the team only turn into dollars if you'd otherwise have hired someone or sent the work out.

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:40.980
Bitfury says it would need at least three more lawyers without Harvey.

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:45.380
That's a hire it imagines avoiding, not a cost it removed.

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:51.480
So plainly, the mechanism is credible, the units are missing,

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:55.120
and the savings are estimates against an imagined baseline.

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:57.600
That's where the evidence has to start.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:02.140
Let's separate what's established from what's claimed.

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:03.440
Established.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:08.040
Three named customers, Bitfury, ICL Group, and SUSE,

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:11.220
use Harvey on real matters and say so publicly.

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:15.460
Companies rarely put their names to a vendor story they don't believe.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:19.460
Establish that they say it, yes.

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:24.280
It's still self-reported, published by the vendor and chosen by the vendor.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:29.640
We don't know how many customers were asked and said no or saw no change at all.

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:36.220
Harvey's longer report, The New Economics of In-House Legal, adds a little.

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:42.020
It draws on a survey of 25 in-house legal teams and 22 in-depth interviews.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:48.100
Harvey commissioned it, and a research firm called RSGI ran it in June 2026.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:51.600
The report doesn't say whether the people in it were Harvey customers.

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:57.180
So it's a customer survey with a research firm's name on it.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:06.660
The headline findings are that 92% report faster turnaround and 65% saw measurable benefits within a month.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:09.220
Neither of those is a spend number.

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:13.180
Fair, now the independent data.

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:18.080
The best source is LexisNexis, Counseling's annual trends report.

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:24.560
In its words, it's built on actual invoices paid to law firms for work completed in 2025.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:34.680
five that's the data set I'd want what does it show not a collapse outside council fees kept

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:42.260
rising across practice areas and regions big law about 100 firms holds a 51.2 percent share

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:50.360
of wallet alternative fee arrangements average just 8.3 percent of matters so in the invoice

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:55.220
data the billable hour is fine and the biggest firms hold the largest share

00:16:55.220 --> 00:17:01.220
that's hard to square with drops of 20 to 50 percent unless those drops sit in

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:07.600
small teams doing routine work which is exactly what the stories describe

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:13.580
routine drafting first-pass diligence local counsel for research much of that

00:17:13.580 --> 00:17:18.740
may sit with mid-sized firms if routine work leaves first big laws share could

00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:22.740
rise even while total spend falls.

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:26.740
Possible, but you've just built that theory to rescue the claim.

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:33.740
Also, CouncilLink covers invoices from 2025, and much of this adoption is more recent.

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:39.740
The invoice data may simply be too early to show anything either way.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:45.740
Agreed. Timing cuts both ways. Now the surveys of general counsel.

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:48.020
Only 20% plan to cut it.

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:53.680
That's a survey of intentions taken in late 2025.

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:57.460
But no vendor ran it, and it points the other way.

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:04.160
CLOC's 2026 State of the Industry report shows where things are moving.

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:10.180
Rising legal demand is outpacing budget and staffing growth, forcing an operational shift.

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:15.080
Only 37% now expect outside counsel spend to rise.

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:18.080
A year earlier, it was 58%.

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:25.700
I'll give you that one. It's a real shift in intent. But it could be budget pressure, not AI.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:35.520
CLOC also found that expected increases in inside spending fell from 65% to 47%. Everyone is tightening.

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:43.880
Fair point. If inside budgets are tightening too, it isn't clearly a story about moving work in-house.

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:46.120
It may just be less money everywhere.

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:52.320
Then there's a survey published by the Association of Corporate Counsel.

00:18:53.020 --> 00:19:02.280
Of 657 in-house professionals, 59% reported no noticeable savings yet from their law firm's use of AI.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:06.300
That measures something different, though.

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:09.760
It's about firms passing on their own AI efficiencies.

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:13.320
Harvey's claim is that clients do the work themselves.

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:18.500
True, and it actually helps Harvey's pitch.

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:24.220
If firms won't pass savings on, clients have a reason to bring work in-house, but it still

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:30.200
means none of the independent data shows the money moving yet.

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:32.500
So here's where the evidence leaves us.

00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:33.660
Established.

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:39.040
The workflow is real and customers believe it saves money on specific matters.

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:40.720
Claimed but not shown.

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:43.480
a measured fall in outside counsel invoices,

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:46.060
adjusted for matter mix at any customer.

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:50.600
How do the different readings take these same facts?

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:53.020
Start with the AGI forward reading.

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:58.680
On that view, Bitfury's 80% draft is an early snapshot of a steep curve.

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:02.260
Models get better at drafting and diligence every year,

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:05.020
so less and less work needs to go outside.

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:09.740
The strongest version of that is about the baseline.

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:16.800
If a three-lawyer team can support global deals, the right comparison isn't last year's invoices.

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:22.260
It's the larger team and the outside counsel the company never had to pay for.

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:31.520
And on that reading, invoice totals will understate the effect, because growing companies spend more on legal no matter what.

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:34.880
The saving shows up as costs that never arrive.

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:39.440
The AI Realist reading is closer to mine.

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:45.440
The independent invoice data shows fees rising and big law holding over half the wallet.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:51.300
The vendor data is three hand-picked accounts and a survey of 25 customers.

00:20:52.060 --> 00:20:56.240
Until those two meet, it's a marketing story with plausible mechanics.

00:20:57.840 --> 00:21:01.320
A realist would also ask what the tool really replaces.

00:21:02.200 --> 00:21:04.120
Outside counsel isn't just ours.

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:09.680
It's judgment, accountability, and a firm whose insurance stands behind the advice.

00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:27.680
Which is what Bitfury describes. Outside firms now review instead of drafting. That's real, but review is often expensive partner time. The cheap associate hours go first, so the dollar saving may be smaller than the hours suggest.

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:33.060
Then there's the market reading, which asks who keeps the savings.

00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:35.880
Law firms are adopting AI, too.

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:43.440
If they keep billing by the hour while their costs fall, the efficiency shows up in firm margins, not client invoices.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:50.780
And alternative fees at 8.3% of matters suggest billing hasn't moved much.

00:21:51.260 --> 00:21:52.880
So clients have two routes.

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:57.260
Bring the work in-house with a tool or push firms onto fixed fees.

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:00.860
Both squeeze the billable hour from different sides.

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:05.280
There's also a quieter liability angle.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:08.840
When an in-house team relies on a model's first draft,

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:12.500
the company carries the risk of a mistake a firm used to carry.

00:22:13.180 --> 00:22:16.140
That cost appears in none of these savings estimates.

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:19.340
I agree with that.

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:23.320
The readings disagree less about the facts than about the baseline.

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:27.100
The forward reading compares against an imagined world,

00:22:27.100 --> 00:22:29.860
the realist against last year's invoices.

00:22:30.460 --> 00:22:32.100
They're measuring different things.

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:35.060
So are Reed.

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:38.040
We discount the 20-50% figures

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:41.340
until a customer shows outside council invoices

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:44.460
before and after, adjusted for matter mix.

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:47.900
Three hand-picked accounts estimating their reliance

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:50.080
is closer to a survey than a measurement.

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:54.540
But we still think outside council spend

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:56.500
is the right line to sell against.

00:22:57.100 --> 00:23:03.020
It's real money, it sits on the profit and loss statement, and the CFO already tracks it.

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:08.300
For builders, there's a product hiding inside the proof problem.

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:17.460
A company that can measure the shift, matching matters to invoices and estimating what work would have cost outside, may win the procurement conversation.

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:20.760
Whoever audits the spend may end up owning the budget.

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:27.720
which gives an edge to the e-billing and spend management platforms that already hold the

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:34.200
invoices a seed stage company would need integrations or a partnership to get at that data

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:42.440
for investors one question worth exploring in diligence might be this can any customer show

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:49.080
outside council invoices for the same matter types for 12 months before and 12 months after adoption

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:52.660
and a follow-up we'd want answered.

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:56.100
Do renewals actually rest on that measured number

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:59.160
or on hours saved and user satisfaction?

00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:04.000
If it's satisfaction, the tool is priced as productivity software,

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:06.060
whatever the pitch deck says.

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:10.580
That test applies far beyond legal.

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:14.380
Any vertical AI company claiming to replace contractors,

00:24:15.040 --> 00:24:18.480
agencies or consultants is selling against a visible cost

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:20.520
and an invisible counterfactual.

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:35.220
Here's where we still disagree, Bella. I think capacity gains without a falling invoice mean buyers are paying for an easier workload, not lower costs. That's a smaller and more crowded market.

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:38.600
I read it more generously.

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:46.760
CLOCs drop from 58% to 37%, expecting higher outside spend looks like early evidence that

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:47.860
intent is changing.

00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:54.620
And single matter estimates like SUSCs and ICLs are the kind legal ops teams actually

00:24:54.620 --> 00:24:55.920
use to approve budgets.

00:24:57.700 --> 00:25:03.540
Intent isn't spend, but I'll grant that single matter evidence beats the headline percentages.

00:25:05.220 --> 00:25:07.440
Then we agree on the hierarchy.

00:25:08.220 --> 00:25:11.940
Invoices adjusted for matter mix beat single matter estimates.

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:17.680
Those beat reliance percentages, and reliance percentages beat satisfaction surveys.

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:20.820
What would settle it?

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:27.020
First, a customer or better an independent auditor publishing outside counsel invoices

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:30.520
before and after adoption, broken out by matter type.

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:34.520
One clean case would be worth more than a stack of testimonials.

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:39.680
Second, the next counseling trends report.

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:44.540
If it keeps this year's timing, it should land next spring and cover invoices paid in

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:46.340
2026.

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:53.900
Watch whether routine matter hours fall and whether Big Law's 51.2% share keeps climbing.

00:25:53.900 --> 00:26:00.600
Third, the next round of general counsel surveys from CLOC and Thomson Reuters.

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:05.480
If fewer expect higher outside spend while inside budgets hold, that looks like work

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:07.120
moving in-house.

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:11.880
If both fall together, it's just belt tightening.

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:14.240
And one more from me, pricing.

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:19.320
If legal AI vendors start charging a share of the outside spend they save, they'd have

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:20.740
to measure it.

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:25.200
That would show they believe their own numbers.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:27.000
What would change our view?

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:32.220
Under-adjusted invoices showing even a 20% drop would make us take the headlines seriously.

00:26:33.020 --> 00:26:37.520
Routine matter hours at firms rising despite adoption would make us doubt it.

00:26:39.160 --> 00:26:43.300
Until then, it's a plausible mechanism with estimated savings.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:46.540
Worth tracking, not yet worth underwriting.

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:49.760
Here's Stephen's take.

00:26:50.480 --> 00:26:54.620
Superpowering experts is what AI does consultants are the opposite path.

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:59.400
That's the deep dive and today's AI briefing

